    "math_academy_id": "q-143098",
    "knowledge_point_id": "031f0fd6-385d-56b8-9e8e-b264e6e89ed3",
    "knowledge_point": "Identifying the Graph of a Non-Strict Inequality Given in Standard Form",
    "problem": "Which of the following shaded areas describes the region bounded by the inequality $3x-2y\\ge 6?$",
    "worked_solution": "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-2y & \\ge 6 \\\\ -2y & \\ge -3x+6 \\\\ y & \\le \\frac{3}{2}x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{2}x-3.\n$$\n\n Since the inequality involves a 'less than *or equal to*' sign, the line $y=\\frac{3}{2}x-3$ itself is part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a **solid line**.\n\nThen, we need to decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality \n\n$$\ny\\le \\frac{3}{2}x-3\n$$\n\n refers to the $y$-values that are *less than* or equal to those on the line $y=\\frac{3}{2}x-3,$ so we shade *below* the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/ea12ed8d62304ecdc90859d5cd206045cef852522ce16c67a9307ddbd755b3dc.png)",
